---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Identity Verification">
  <h1>Identity Verification (KYC)</h1>
  <p>
    Identity supports document + selfie verification through a pluggable
    provider interface. The first shipping implementation is
    <strong>Azure AI Face Liveness</strong>; deployments can wire any other
    provider (Onfido, Persona, Veriff, Sumsub, an in-house pipeline) by
    implementing the small <code>idv.Provider</code> interface and
    selecting it via config.
  </p>

  <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4 text-sm">
    <strong>Optional or mandatory.</strong> The feature works in two modes:
    <em>optional</em> (default) lets clients run verification at any time
    and exposes the result on <code>User.idv_verified</code>;
    <em>mandatory</em> blocks login for users whose latest verification
    has not reached <code>APPROVED</code>. Mirror of the existing
    <code>email_verified</code> pattern.
  </div>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>

  <h3>Pick a provider</h3>
  <Code code={`# Disabled (default) — RPCs return CodeUnimplemented
-e GATEWAY_IDV_PROVIDER=

# Stub — auto-approves every session, useful for bring-up only
-e GATEWAY_IDV_PROVIDER=stub

# Azure AI Face Liveness
-e GATEWAY_IDV_PROVIDER=azure
-e GATEWAY_IDV_AZURE_ENDPOINT=https://my-face.cognitiveservices.azure.com
-e GATEWAY_IDV_AZURE_KEY=...subscription-key...
-e GATEWAY_IDV_AZURE_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=600`} lang="bash" />

  <h3>Gate login on verification</h3>
  <Code code={`# Reject login when latest verification != APPROVED
-e GATEWAY_IDV_REQUIRED=true`} lang="bash" />

  <p>
    With <code>GATEWAY_IDV_REQUIRED=true</code>, both
    <code>PasswordLogin</code> and the <code>OAuthLogin</code>
    finalisation return <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION:
    identity verification required</code> until the user has an approved
    verification. Clients route those errors to the verification flow.
  </p>

  <h2>Flow</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      Client calls <code>BeginIdentityVerification</code>. Server creates
      a session with the configured provider, persists a PENDING record
      against the caller, and returns a provider-specific
      <code>session_token</code> the client SDK uses for capture.
    </li>
    <li>
      Client SDK drives document + selfie capture. For Azure, this is the
      <em>Vision SDK</em> initialised with the returned token.
    </li>
    <li>
      Client polls <code>GetIdentityVerificationStatus</code>. Server
      refreshes from the provider, persists terminal verdicts, and on
      <code>APPROVED</code> sets <code>User.idv_verified = true</code>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Begin RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`message BeginIdentityVerificationRequest {}

message BeginIdentityVerificationResponse {
  string verification_id = 1;
  string provider        = 2;  // "azure", "stub", ...
  string session_token   = 3;  // Pass to the provider's client SDK
  google.protobuf.Timestamp expires_at = 4;
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Status RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`message GetIdentityVerificationStatusRequest {
  // Optional. When empty, the latest verification for the caller is returned.
  string verification_id = 1;
}

message GetIdentityVerificationStatusResponse {
  IdentityVerification verification = 1;
}

enum IdentityVerificationStatus {
  IDENTITY_VERIFICATION_STATUS_UNSPECIFIED = 0;
  IDENTITY_VERIFICATION_STATUS_PENDING     = 1; // Client has not submitted yet
  IDENTITY_VERIFICATION_STATUS_IN_REVIEW   = 2; // Submitted, awaiting verdict
  IDENTITY_VERIFICATION_STATUS_APPROVED    = 3;
  IDENTITY_VERIFICATION_STATUS_REJECTED    = 4;
  IDENTITY_VERIFICATION_STATUS_EXPIRED     = 5;
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>User flag</h2>
  <p>
    Once a verification reaches <code>APPROVED</code>, the user record
    carries <code>idv_verified = true</code> and
    <code>idv_verified_at</code> (epoch ms). These survive across new
    verification attempts; the latest verdict overrides only on a new
    successful poll.
  </p>

  <h2>Plugging in your own provider</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>pkg/idv</code> package defines a minimal interface; any
    deployment can drop in a custom implementation:
  </p>
  <Code code={`type Provider interface {
    Name() string
    BeginVerification(ctx context.Context, req Request) (*Session, error)
    GetVerification(ctx context.Context, providerSessionID string) (*StatusResult, error)
}`} lang="go" />

  <p>
    A non-Azure provider then registers itself in <code>identityserver/adapters.go</code>'s
    <code>buildIDVProvider</code> switch (or in a fork's equivalent
    factory). The service layer, persistence, RPC contract, and login
    gate all keep working unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Operational notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Sync vs async</strong>: Azure is a sync flow — the server
      polls the provider on each <code>GetIdentityVerificationStatus</code>
      call. Async providers (e.g. Onfido webhooks) implement the same
      interface and update local state from a webhook handler; the
      polling RPC then returns whatever local state is fresh.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cost</strong>: Azure Face Liveness is billed per session.
      Set <code>GATEWAY_IDV_AZURE_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS</code> tightly
      enough that abandoned sessions don't accumulate cost.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stub provider</strong>: never deploy with
      <code>GATEWAY_IDV_PROVIDER=stub</code> — it auto-approves every
      session. The binary logs a loud warning at startup when stub is
      selected.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
